+{"status": "pending", "primary_model": "glm-5.3-flash", "primary_provider": "zai-glm-default", "created_at": "2026-09-21T11:23:44+00:00", "attempts": 0, "id": "TJ-GAP-072", "priority": "P3", "title": "[P3] Upgrade never prunes: the documented upgrade path (re-running install.sh) cp -R's the plugin tree with no manifest/prune, so files deleted upstream persist forever in ~/.local/lib/terminal-jail, and differing-rule .bak-* backups accumulate unbounded", "detail": "install.sh:555 is a bare `cp -R \"$SCRIPT_DIR/plugin/terminal_jail\" \"$LIB_DIR/plugin/\"` — no manifest, no rsync --delete, no wipe-and-recreate; grep of install.sh finds no prune mechanism anywhere. After upgrading from a checkout where a module was renamed/removed, the installed tree keeps the stale file and the bridge's candidate resolution can still import it, making installed-state vs checkout-state indistinguishable when debugging. Separately, install.sh:586-591 backs up user-differing rules as .bak-<timestamp> with no retention cap.", "reasoning": "stand-in PM cycle 2026-09-21 (hunter-verified real-use finding; PM re-ran every load-bearing probe before gating)", "review_notes": "Pick one: (a) record an install manifest (list of files install.sh wrote) and prune manifest entries that vanish from the checkout on re-install; or (b) wipe-and-recreate LIB_DIR/plugin/terminal_jail on re-install after the fresh copy succeeds (copy to tmp dir, then atomic swap), with the same .bak protection rules already carry. Cap or prune .bak-* retention (e.g. keep newest 5). PASS: install, delete a module from plugin/terminal_jail in the checkout, re-run install.sh, and the file is absent from the installed lib tree; .bak count is bounded after repeated installs with changing user rules.", "source": "pm-standin-2026-09-21"}
